<span id="A1.T7.4.8.1.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_top">
<span id="A1.T7.4.8.1.1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.T7.4.8.1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:70%;">This patient is showing signs of schizophrenia, indicated by his disheveled appearance, flat affect, and auditory hallucinations, along with a recent sudden behavioral change characterized by increased talkativeness, grandiosity, and agitation. The question asks about predictors of good prognosis in schizophrenia. Prognostic factors generally include the presence of affective symptoms, good social support, and an acute onset, while negative symptoms (such as flat affect and social withdrawal) and male sex are typically associated with a poorer prognosis. Being single is not a strong predictor of prognosis by itself.</span></span>
