<td id="A1.T7.4.18.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left">
<span id="A1.T7.4.18.1.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_top">
<span id="A1.T7.4.18.1.1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.T7.4.18.1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:70%;">The patient presents with an itchy papulopustular eruption on the trunk, back, and buttocks, with inflammation of hair follicles, which suggests folliculitis. The history of recent swimming or exposure to contaminated water, along with culture findings of motile, gram-negative rods that are oxidase positive and non-lactose fermenting, strongly suggests Pseudomonas aeruginosa as the causative organism. Pseudomonas aeruginosa is well-known for producing a characteristic blue-green pigment (pyocyanin). This pigment is a hallmark in culture and helps in identifying this bacterium.</span></span>
</span></td></tr>
<tr id="A1.T7.4.19" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="A1.T7.4.19.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left">
